      "type": "word-problem",
      "question_html": "A radioactive isotope starts at 2,000 grams and has a half-life of 8 years.<br>(a) Write an exponential decay model <em>A</em>(<em>t</em>) for the amount remaining after <em>t</em> years.<br>(b) Using your model, how many years until only 125 grams remain?",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "q15_model",
          "type": "text",
          "label": "A(t) ="
        },
        {
          "id": "q15_t",
          "type": "number",
          "label": "t =",
          "answer": 32,
          "tolerance": 0.5
        }
      ],
      "hint": "A(t) = A₀ · r^(t/T). Decay = multiply by 0.5 per half-life. Half-life T = 8 years. Then set A(t) = 125.",
      "solution_steps": [
        "A₀ = 2000 (starting amount). Decay factor r = 0.5 (half-life). Period T = 8 years.",
        "Model: A(t) = 2000 · 0.5^(t/8).",
        "Set 2000 · 0.5^(t/8) = 125 → 0.5^(t/8) = 0.0625.",
        "0.0625 = 0.5⁴, so t/8 = 4 → t = 32 years.",
        "Check: 2000 · 0.5⁴ = 2000 · 0.0625 = 125 ✓"
      ],
      "feedback_correct": "🔥 32 years. Exponential decay.",
      "feedback_wrong": "Divide by 2000 first. Then: 0.5^? = 0.0625."
    }
